# -- end of source
# f1 = byName, f2 = byValue, f3 = handed, f4 = Knob, f5 = turn, f6 = spin
# `this.turn = this.turn.bind(this)`: the name lies in a field that Link appends (its own test: a-name-that-the-constructor-of-a-class-stores-over-its-own-method-lies-in-a-field.ir).
# What is held in that field, and in a variable read out of it, is a bound function of the method, beside every primitive (the result of a call of `bind` that nobody has proven) and beside the method itself.
# Of what is expected of a CALLEE only the objects count; one part of bound functions is asked for first, in front of the one function that Link lists at the call by name.
# So the call by name, the call by value and the call off `this` ask the cell, in front of their loops, and enter the method: the sums are of whole numbers.
